From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 16:08:32 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] ntdll: Implement the AVL generic tables and
 RtlIsNameInExpression.

Office's App-V subsystem (AppvIsvSubsystems64.dll, loaded into Word
and the other applications) keeps its tables in AVL generic tables and
matches names with RtlIsNameInExpression.  The AVL functions Wine had
did nothing: the table was not initialized, an insert neither
allocated nor returned the element, a lookup found nothing; the
enumeration, the delete and RtlIsNameInExpression it also imports
were missing or raised.

The tables now follow what Windows was measured doing
(tools/avlprobe): the element's data follows its links, the tree
hangs off the right of BalancedRoot, whose balance stops the
rebalancing walks, a node with two children gives its place to the
next or the previous one from its deeper side, a delete moves a
restart key it removes to the previous element, inserts and deletes
drop the element cursor, and RtlEnumerateGenericTableLikeADirectory
continues from its restart key only while nothing was deleted.  The
compare routine gets the caller's buffer first.

RtlIsNameInExpression keeps the set of states of the expression that
match the name so far, as the NT file systems do, which matches all
2160 combinations of wildcard expressions and names the probe tried on
Windows.  ntoskrnl's FsRtlIsNameInExpression, a stub returning FALSE,
uses it.  The new tests pass on Windows 11 build 29671.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit c92e5b9cdacc9525c31689d9612c39484cb9916e)

